            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the two exterior doors. I recommend replacing the weather stripping along both exterior doors, as well as adjusting the door sweep to create a better seal. Electrical/light switch outlet gaskets are easy to install, as these are simply placed over the outlet behind the plastic cover. These should be installed on any outlets/switches located on an exterior wall of the home. 



Longer term upgrades, such as wall insualtion and new windows, will help address other air leakage paths found within the building envelope. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, this includes adding 2 inches to the lower level walls and concrete foundation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1450&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;49.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>

The exposed floor of the addition of your home appear to be 2x10 joists with 10 inches (R28) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation. 

&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates available for adding less than R20 insulation to exposed floors. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</exposedFloors>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older triple pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 190&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;















&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Furnace&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 85.1% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1150&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;31.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

If you decideto install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By installing a ULC Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Furnace Fresh Air Supply&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



As discussed, there is a damper located on the fresh air supply pipe that you can manually adjust, this is connected to the duct work of your furnace. If you find there is consistently excess humidity in the house, you will want to open the fresh air damper further. If you find the house is consistently becoming too dry, than you will want to close the fresh air damper further.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



